WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.120 --> 00:04.300
Our world is complex and it's changing fast.

2
00:04.360 --> 00:11.580
And in today's workplace, we are called to see opportunities for innovation
and also watch out for threats,

3
00:11.640 --> 00:16.620
draw on different capabilities and expertise to solve complex challenges,

4
00:16.680 --> 00:19.900
and make rapid decisions with many trade-offs.

5
00:19.960 --> 00:23.740
Well, all this isn't an easy job for one person to tackle,

6
00:23.800 --> 00:27.420
which is why teams have the potential to be so powerful.

7
00:27.480 --> 00:36.780
Teams can make real breakthroughs because they have the potential
to adapt to the world around them better and faster than any individual on their own.

8
00:36.840 --> 00:39.180
But not all teams do this well.

9
00:39.240 --> 00:43.340
Think of the teams you worked with that were high-performing and enjoyable.

10
00:43.880 --> 00:46.460
Now think of the teams that weren't.

11
00:46.600 --> 00:54.825
At some point in our life, we've all been on a team
where we may have felt there was shaming and blaming team members for their mistakes.

12
00:54.885 --> 00:59.385
We might have even sensed an unsettling tension around voicing different opinions

13
00:59.445 --> 01:02.985
and a lack of desire to help each other learn and grow.

14
01:03.045 --> 01:08.985
We probably describe it as an environment
where we didn't feel comfortable showing up as our full selves.

15
01:09.045 --> 01:13.225
We might have felt the need to censor our ways in order to avoid backlash.

16
01:13.285 --> 01:18.905
Or maybe we just weren't that invested
and so didn't participate as much as usual.

17
01:18.965 --> 01:23.625
On the other hand, the most effective teams work and learn collaboratively,

18
01:23.685 --> 01:27.865
building on each other's strengths and offsetting each other's weaknesses.

19
01:27.925 --> 01:32.025
And to do that well, teams need psychological safety.

20
01:32.165 --> 01:37.488
When we feel psychologically safe, we are willing to be creative and take risks,

21
01:37.548 --> 01:39.808
bring our authentic selves to work,

22
01:39.868 --> 01:43.408
know that expressing ideas and concerns is welcomed,

23
01:43.468 --> 01:46.768
aren't afraid to admit mistakes and learn from them,

24
01:46.828 --> 01:51.568
and believe we're valuable team members, even when we ask for help.

25
01:51.628 --> 01:57.088
It's well researched that psychological safety is an important driver of performance.

26
01:57.148 --> 02:04.448
Yet research has also found that probably less than a third of teams
actually experience psychological safety.

27
02:04.668 --> 02:08.688
Imagine the benefits if we doubled or tripled that number.

28
02:08.748 --> 02:14.608
Research suggests that doubling psychological safety would not only increase productivity,

29
02:14.668 --> 02:21.648
it would reduce safety incidents and turnover,
helping to save money and improve employee well-being.

30
02:22.083 --> 02:28.023
Other studies have found that hospital units with more error reporting had fewer deaths.

31
02:28.083 --> 02:32.903
Why? Because when people hide errors, more mistakes happen.

32
02:32.963 --> 02:41.383
The same is true in environments like manufacturing and mining,
where recognizing mistakes has a direct impact on physical safety.

33
02:41.443 --> 02:49.303
More broadly, in psychologically safe environments,
employees perform better and they're more satisfied with their jobs.

34
02:49.363 --> 02:58.743
Would you like to work on a team where you felt you could be your most comfortable,
competent, creative, risk-taking, mistake-making self?

35
02:58.803 --> 03:03.760
It's within your power to help make this happen,
no matter the role you play on a team.

36
03:03.820 --> 03:08.320
You have an opportunity to create a psychologically safe environment.

37
03:08.380 --> 03:14.500
And in doing so, you can help your team be greater than the sum of its parts.
